wf.py: Your approach to opening using with open, I like.  Your logic works and the step-by-step comments make it easy to follow, but a beginner improvement would be avoiding variable names like list and set (they overwrite Python built-ins).
xy.py: with open - I discoverd this too, nice use.  Nice job organizing your code with a function, clear comments, and regex!!! I wanted to try it.  You rock. Oh and yea with set to solve the problem deduping cleanly - my fav; as a small improvement, catching specific exceptions (like FileNotFoundError) instead of a broad Exception would be more precise for debugging - but Brick said no bueno, so, yea.
md.py: This is a clean solution with (what I think is the ) correct use of set, and interesting use of lowercase. one small improvement is printing a short label with the result so users know what the number means.
gs.py: Might be good to add what the number is about on the output! Nice concise program with solid exception handling and lowercase normalization, but using strip(string.punctuation) only removes punctuation at the ends of words, so words with internal punctuation (like can't) may be handled differently than expected.
tf.py: Strong structure with main and then another function, and regex - I wish I had used this. 
gx.py: You used good error handling and clear processing steps, but printing every word/count changes the assignment goal. 
mj.py: You have a clear overall approach and good comments, but there are a few beginner fixes needed: the argument-check block is duplicated, and using with open(pathname) as file: would simplify file closing and make the code cleaner.